WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.829
A payment authorization has five fields, and its schema gives each one a number, from 1 to 5.

2
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.737
Protobuf never writes the names.

3
00:00:11.737 --> 00:00:14.944
For each field, it writes a key, then the value.

4
00:00:14.944 --> 00:00:17.215
This authorization takes 42 bytes.

5
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:19.910
Take the amount, 4,000 cents.

6
00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:22.628
Its key is one byte: 18 in hex.

7
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:25.601
In bits, the lowest three are the wire type.

8
00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:28.747
Here it's 0, a varint, which holds a whole number.

9
00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:31.515
The bits above them are the field number, 3.

10
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:35.912
A varint keeps 7 bits of the number in each byte, lowest bits first.

11
00:00:35.912 --> 00:00:39.595
The top bit of each byte says whether another byte follows.

12
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.759
a0 has its top bit set, so another byte follows.

13
00:00:43.759 --> 00:00:45.573
Its low 7 bits are 32.

14
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:50.681
1f has a top bit of 0, so it's the last byte.

15
00:00:50.681 --> 00:00:56.907
It holds 31, and comes second, so it counts 31 times 128.

16
00:00:56.907 --> 00:00:59.419
32 plus 3,968 is 4,000.

17
00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:02.673
Now the risk team adds field 6, a risk score.

18
00:01:02.673 --> 00:01:08.009
The payment service starts writing it: two more bytes, a key and the score, 87.

19
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.625
The settlement service still runs old code that has never heard of field 6.

20
00:01:13.625 --> 00:01:17.024
It reads the key, hex 30: field 6, wire type 0.

21
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:24.530
Wire type 0 says a varint comes next, so the old code reads it and keeps the bytes aside, as an unknown field.

22
00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.511
When it writes the record back, the risk score goes with it.

23
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:34.689
A year later, someone moves the amount to number 8, and gives number 3 to a new field, the fee.

24
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:39.568
The payment service writes a fee of 150 cents under number 3.

25
00:01:39.568 --> 00:01:41.109
Its key is 18 again.

26
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.649
The old code still thinks 3 is the amount.

27
00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:49.136
It reads 150 cents instead of 4,000, with no error at all.

28
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:51.547
On the wire, the number is the field.

29
00:01:51.547 --> 00:01:55.197
Never change a field's number, and never reuse a deleted one.

30
00:01:55.197 --> 00:01:58.548
Reserve it instead, and give the new field a new number.
